<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corinthia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corinthia is a region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> with the mainland. Dominated by ancient Corinth and the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a>, it controlled the land route into the peninsula and the trade of two gulfs. In the early 13th century it was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-sgouros">Leo Sgouros</a> before falling to the Frankish conquerors, and it is now a regional unit of Greece.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corinthia.jpg" alt="Corinthia" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Korinthia_municipalities_numbered.svg: Badseed derivative work: Pitichinaccio (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010_Dimi_Korinthias_numbered.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
